  "output": "We are given: \"Jerry has a flock of chickens. The red chickens produce 3 eggs a day, and the white chickens produce 5 eggs a day. Every day Jerry collects 42 eggs. If he has two more white chickens than red chickens, how many red chickens does he have?\"\n\nLet r = number of red chickens, w = number of white chickens.\nGiven: red produce 3 eggs/day, white produce 5 eggs/day.\nTotal eggs per day = 3r + 5w = 42.\nAlso: \"he has two more white chickens than red chickens\" => w = r + 2.\nWe need to find r.\n\nSubstitute w = r + 2 into the equation:\n3r + 5(r + 2) = 42\n3r + 5r + 10 = 42\n8r + 10 = 42\n8r = 32\nr = 4.\n\nSo red chickens = 4.\n\nCheck: r=4, w=6. Eggs: 4*3=12, 6*5=30, total 42. Correct.\n\nFinal answer: 4.</think>Let \\( r \\) be the number of red chickens and \\( w \\) be the number of white chickens.  \nGiven:  \n- Red chickens produce 3 eggs per day.  \n- White chickens produce 5 eggs per day.  \n- Total eggs collected per day: \\( 3r + 5w = 42 \\).  \n- White chickens are two more than red chickens: \\( w = r + 2 \\).  \n\nSubstitute \\( w = r + 2 \\) into the egg equation:  \n\\[ 3r + 5(r + 2) = 42 \\]  \n\\[ 3r + 5r + 10 = 42 \\]  \n\\[ 8r + 10 = 42 \\]  \n\\[ 8r = 32 \\]  \n\\[ r = 4 \\]  \n\nThus, Jerry has 4 red chickens.\n\nFINAL: 4",
